# Copy this file to .env.local (gitignored, see .gitignore's "# Env"
# section) and fill in real values. Originally used only by ops scripts
# under infrastructure/scripts/ that need to call the Cloudflare REST
# API directly (Workers AI / Vectorize) from a plain Node process
# outside the Workers runtime -- there is no live AI/VECTORIZE binding
# available there, same reasoning infrastructure/scripts/lib/d1-exec.mjs
# documents for why D1 access goes through `wrangler d1 execute`
# instead. Workers AI and Vectorize have no equivalent
# `wrangler ai run`/`wrangler vectorize upsert-via-http` passthrough for
# a one-off text embed from a script, so a raw Cloudflare API token is
# unavoidable there (confirmed against
# developers.cloudflare.com/workers-ai/models/bge-base-en-v1.5's own
# curl example and ArxivExplorer's scripts/debug-vectorize.ts, same
# account, same pattern -- no bespoke admin-secret layer invented here).
#
# Widened 2026-08-02 (ROADMAP.md Milestone J CI item) to also carry
# "D1: Edit" permission, so this same token now doubles as wrangler's
# non-interactive auth for `wrangler d1 execute --remote` -- both
# locally (this file) and in CI, where it's exported as
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (wrangler's standard env var name, distinct from
# this file's own CF_TOKEN name) from the same underlying GitHub secret
# value. Locally, D1 access still normally piggybacks on wrangler's own
# browser-login session and doesn't need this var set -- but wrangler
# will use CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN over the cached login if both are
# present, so don't be surprised if setting this in your shell changes
# which credential a local `wrangler d1 execute --remote` call uses.
#
# Create/edit a token at https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens
# with "Workers AI: Edit", "Vectorize: Edit", and "D1: Edit" permissions
# scoped to this account, nothing broader.
CF_TOKEN=
